<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acapulco</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Acapulco is a port on the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> it was the American terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleons</a>, which carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and returned with Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and other Asian goods. This route linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s American colonies to Asian trade for about two and a half centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acapulco.jpg" alt="Acapulco" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mitrush · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyscrapers_in_Acapulco_Diamante.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Spanish silver dollar, or piece of eight, circulated from Europe to China, especially after the Manila galleons began sailing between <b>Acapulco</b> and Manila in 1565.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Manila galleons sailed between <b>Acapulco</b> and Manila for 250 years, from 1565 to 1815, carrying Mexican silver to Asia and returning with Chinese silk and porcelain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1565 to 1815 the Manila Galleon linked <b>Acapulco</b> and Manila, the first sustained direct trade link between the Americas and Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Silver travelled by the Armada del Mar del Sur from Callao to Panama and on to Seville, and by way of <b>Acapulco</b> and the Manila galleons to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Countless place names in Mexico and Central America are Nahuatl: Mexico itself, Guatemala, <b>Acapulco</b>, Oaxaca and the volcano Popocatépetl, “smoking mountain”.</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>The Manila galleons carried Chinese silk and porcelain to <b>Acapulco</b> for Mexican silver from 1565 to 1815.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
